The new LDA overfits (held-out hit 44%), but the old phase direction w7 generalizes better. At the held-out negatives' 99.9th percentile it flags 79% of degenerate positions. The negatives are dominated by thinking positions, though. The risky negatives are first answer segments of normal answers (c = 1), and the positions that matter are those just before a 3rd-or-later close. This block breaks the w7 score down by group on all 40 texts: thinking, first answer segment (single-close and multi-close texts separately), later segments, and the pre-close positions for close #2, #3, #4 and beyond. If first-segment positions stay well below the pre-close positions for close #4 and beyond, w7 can serve as the flag together with the drive.

```python
# Hypothesis: w7 separates pre-close positions of late re-closes from first answer segments (the real negatives), not just from thinking
w = w7n
groups = {k: [] for k in ['think','ans1_single','ans1_multi','seg2','seg3','seg4+']}
pre = {2: [], 3: [], 4: [], 5: [], '6+': []}
for i in range(40):
    n = lens[i]; tk = np.asarray(toks4[i,:n]); s = X[i] @ w
    pos_, neg_, c, d, t0 = labels_for(i)
    tot = int((tk == THINK_END).sum())
    groups['think'].append(s[(c == 0) & (np.arange(n) >= t0)])
    groups['ans1_single' if tot == 1 else 'ans1_multi'].append(s[c == 1])
    groups['seg2'].append(s[c == 2]); groups['seg3'].append(s[c == 3]); groups['seg4+'].append(s[c >= 4])
    for t in np.where(tk == THINK_END)[0]:
        k = int(c[t])  # this close is the k-th
        if k >= 2 and t > 0:
            key = k if k <= 5 else '6+'
            pre[key].append(s[t-1])
for k, v in groups.items():
    v = np.concatenate(v) if v else np.array([])
    if len(v): print(f'{k:12s} n={len(v):6d} p50 {np.median(v):.2f} p90 {np.percentile(v,90):.2f} p99 {np.percentile(v,99):.2f} max {v.max():.2f}')
for k, v in pre.items():
    v = np.array(v)
    if len(v): print(f'pre-close #{k}: n={len(v)} p10 {np.percentile(v,10):.2f} p50 {np.median(v):.2f} min {v.min():.2f}')
```

Normalized w7 rises with the close count, but it does not cleanly separate the positions that matter. First answer segments of normal single-close answers reach p99 2.45 (max 2.95). Pre-close positions of the 4th close have median 2.17, and those of the 6th and later have p10 2.11. On its own, w7 would flag a few percent of normal answer positions. Ending the turn at such a position (mid-code, say) would cost real accuracy.

The blocker would be gated by w7 AND the detector drive, so what matters is the joint distribution. Pass 2 has exact layer-23 activations (including 3109) for 16 texts that should be the first 16 tf3 texts. This block checks that the tokens align, then compares (w7, a3109) across three groups: normal single-close answers, thinking loops, and pre-close positions of late closes. If a corner such as w7 > 2.2 and a3109 > 8 holds late pre-close positions and almost no normal-answer positions, the AND gate is safe.

```python
# Hypothesis: the AND of w7 (count/phase) and 3109 activation (drive) isolates late re-close points from normal answers and thinking loops
m2 = json.load(open('/work/fwd_2/meta.json')); toks2 = np.load('/work/fwd_2/tokens.npy', mmap_mode='r')
A2 = np.load('/work/fwd_2/mlp_act.npy', mmap_mode='r'); print('A2', A2.shape, m2.get('mlp_layer'))
li23 = m2['mlp_layer'].index(23)
align = []
for i in range(16):
    n2 = m2['lengths'][i]; n = min(n2, lens[i])
    align.append(bool(np.array_equal(np.asarray(toks2[i,:n]), np.asarray(toks4[i,:n]))) and n2 >= lens[i]-1)
print('aligned', align)
J = {'normal1': [], 'thinkloop': [], 'pre_late': [], 'pre_23': []}
for i in range(16):
    if not align[i]: continue
    n = lens[i]; tk = np.asarray(toks4[i,:n]); s = X[i] @ w7n; a = np.asarray(A2[i, li23, :n, 3109], dtype=np.float32)
    pos_, neg_, c, d, t0 = labels_for(i); tot = int((tk == THINK_END).sum())
    if tot == 1: J['normal1'].append(np.stack([s[c==1], a[c==1]], 1))
    J['thinkloop'].append(np.stack([s[(c==0)&(np.arange(n)>=t0)], a[(c==0)&(np.arange(n)>=t0)]], 1))
    for t in np.where(tk == THINK_END)[0]:
        k = int(c[t])
        if k >= 4: J['pre_late'].append(np.array([[s[t-1], a[t-1]]]))
        elif k >= 2: J['pre_23'].append(np.array([[s[t-1], a[t-1]]]))
for k in J: J[k] = np.concatenate(J[k]) if J[k] else np.zeros((0,2))
for k, v in J.items():
    if len(v) == 0: print(k, 'none'); continue
    print(f'{k:10s} n={len(v):6d} a3109 p50 {np.median(v[:,1]):.1f} p90 {np.percentile(v[:,1],90):.1f} p99 {np.percentile(v[:,1],99):.1f}', end=' | ')
    for ts, ta in [(2.0, 6), (2.2, 8), (2.4, 8), (2.2, 10)]:
        print(f'w>{ts}&a>{ta}: {((v[:,0]>ts)&(v[:,1]>ta)).mean():.4f}', end=' ')
    print()
```

The AND works well on these 16 texts. Detector drive alone is useless: in normal single-close answers 3109 runs high (median 9.8), because answers copy their drafts. The joint gate "w7 > 2.0 and a3109 > 6" behaves as follows:
- late pre-close positions (close #4 and later): fires at 96%;
- 2nd/3rd closes: about 10%, so early productive restarts are kept;
- thinking loops: 0 of 61,100 positions;
- normal answers: 1.3% of positions.

That last number decides whether ending the turn there is harmful. If the flagged normal positions sit at the very end of the answer (after the final box or fence, where E3 ends the turn anyway), the false positives are harmless. If they sit mid-code or mid-text, the action must be weaker or the threshold higher. This block locates the flagged normal-answer positions in the answer and prints short excerpts. It covers the 16 texts with exact drive, and w7-only positions for single-close answers in all 40 texts.

```python
# Hypothesis: the few normal-answer positions passing the gate sit at the answer's end (after box/fence), where ending is harmless
def dec(ids): return tok.decode([int(x) for x in ids]).encode('ascii','replace').decode().replace('\n','\\n')
for i in range(40):
    n = lens[i]; tk = np.asarray(toks4[i,:n])
    if int((tk == THINK_END).sum()) != 1: continue
    s = X[i] @ w7n; p1 = int(np.where(tk == THINK_END)[0][0]); alen = n - p1
    if i < 16:
        a = np.asarray(A2[i, li23, :n, 3109], dtype=np.float32); fl = np.where((s > 2.0) & (a > 6) & (np.arange(n) > p1))[0]
    else:
        fl = np.where((s > 2.2) & (np.arange(n) > p1))[0]
    if len(fl) == 0: print(i, 'answer len', alen, 'no flagged'); continue
    rel = (fl - p1) / alen
    ended = 'im_end' if IM_END in tk[p1:] else 'no-end'
    print(i, 'ans len', alen, ended, 'flagged', len(fl), 'rel pos p10/p50/p90', np.round(np.percentile(rel,[10,50,90]),2), '| ex:', dec(tk[max(fl[len(fl)//2]-12,0):fl[len(fl)//2]+1])[-90:])
```

The flagged normal-answer positions are spread through the whole answer, mid-text and mid-code (relative position 0.03 to 0.98). Short code answers (texts 37-39) have w7 > 2.2 at up to a quarter of their positions. So with w7 as the count signal, any action that ends the turn would sometimes cut normal answers mid-code. Only "lower `</think>`" is safe with this gate, and that alone does not end degenerate cycles.

I need a cleaner count or recency signal. Late re-closes in degenerate cycles come shortly after the previous close, while in normal answers a recent close only happens in the first tokens of the answer. This block measures two things. First, the gap since the previous close at late pre-close positions. Second, whether any recorded layer (h3, h4, h7, h8, h10-h15, h23) carries a held-out-clean "recent `</think>`" direction (gap ≤ 40 tokens vs > 200 within the answer). If the gaps are short and some layer separates them cleanly, the gate becomes recency AND w7 AND drive.

```python
# Hypothesis: late re-closes follow short segments (small gap), and some layer encodes 'recent </think>' cleanly (held-out)
gaps = []
for i in range(40):
    n = lens[i]; tk = np.asarray(toks4[i,:n]); pc = np.where(tk == THINK_END)[0]
    for j in range(3, len(pc)): gaps.append(pc[j] - pc[j-1])
gaps = np.array(gaps); print('gap before close #4+: n', len(gaps), 'p50', np.median(gaps), 'p90', np.percentile(gaps,90), 'share<=40', (gaps<=40).mean())
H3i = m3['hidden_index']; print('fwd_3 idx', H3i)
def feats(src, li, i):
    arr = H4 if src == 4 else H3
    return rmsn(np.asarray(arr[i, li, :lens[i]], dtype=np.float32))
def rec_labels(i):
    pos_, neg_, c, d, t0 = labels_for(i)
    return np.where((c >= 1) & (d <= 40) & (d >= 1), 1, np.where(((c >= 1) & (d > 200)) | ((c == 0) & (np.arange(lens[i]) >= t0)), 0, -1))
res = {}
for src, idxs in [(4, m4['hidden_index']), (3, H3i)]:
    for li, h in enumerate(idxs):
        Xa = {i: feats(src, li, i) for i in range(40)}
        Ya = {i: rec_labels(i) for i in range(40)}
        P = np.concatenate([Xa[i][Ya[i]==1] for i in tr_ids]); N = np.concatenate([Xa[i][Ya[i]==0] for i in tr_ids])
        Sw = (np.cov(P.T)*len(P) + np.cov(N.T)*len(N))/(len(P)+len(N)); Sw += 0.2*np.trace(Sw)/1024*np.eye(1024)
        wr = np.linalg.solve(Sw, P.mean(0)-N.mean(0)); wr /= np.linalg.norm(wr)
        sp = np.concatenate([Xa[i][Ya[i]==1]@wr for i in te_ids if (Ya[i]==1).any()]); sn = np.concatenate([Xa[i][Ya[i]==0]@wr for i in te_ids])
        thr = np.percentile(sn, 99.9); res[h] = wr
        print(f'h{h}: test pos n={len(sp)} hit@neg99.9 {(sp>thr).mean():.3f}  hit@neg99 {(sp>np.percentile(sn,99)).mean():.3f}')
        del Xa
```

Recency alone is not crisp either. 83% of late re-closes come within 40 tokens of the previous close (median 21), but the best held-out recency readout (h3) reaches only 73% hits at a 0.1% false-positive level. Each soft signal fails somewhere different: w7 is confounded by formal content, recency by answer openings, drive by copying. Their AND may still be clean.

This block tests the combination on all 40 texts. It uses a drive proxy for texts 16-39, calibrated against the exact 3109 activations of texts 0-15: the projection of rms-normalized h23 onto 3109's effective gate direction. Then it scores w7 + β·(h7 recency) at several β and thresholds, with proxy drive > 8. It reports false-positive rates in normal single-close answers and hit rates at late pre-close positions. If some setting reaches about 0% false positives with more than 70% hits, the turn-ending action is safe enough to try. Otherwise only `</think>` suppression is.

```python
# Hypothesis: combining count (w7), recency (h7 rec dir) and drive gives a near-zero-FP gate for late re-closes
L23i = H3i.index(23)
geff = (gate23[3109] * gam23).numpy()
prox = {}; 
for i in range(40):
    prox[i] = rmsn(np.asarray(H3[i, L23i, :lens[i]], dtype=np.float32)) @ geff
xa = np.concatenate([prox[i][:lens[i]] for i in range(16)]); ya = np.concatenate([np.asarray(A2[i, li23, :lens[i], 3109], dtype=np.float32) for i in range(16)])
sel = ya > 1
cal = np.polyfit(xa[sel], ya[sel], 1); print('proxy corr (a>1):', round(np.corrcoef(xa[sel], ya[sel])[0,1],3), 'fit', np.round(cal,3))
drv = {i: (np.asarray(A2[i, li23, :lens[i], 3109], dtype=np.float32) if i < 16 else np.polyval(cal, prox[i])) for i in range(40)}
rec7 = res[7]
R7 = {i: X[i] @ rec7 for i in range(40)}; S7 = {i: X[i] @ w7n for i in range(40)}
def gate_eval(beta, thr, athr=8):
    fpn = 0; nn = 0; hit = 0; nh = 0; fp_think = 0; nt = 0; h23_ = 0; n23 = 0
    for i in range(40):
        n = lens[i]; tk = np.asarray(toks4[i,:n]); c = np.cumsum(tk == THINK_END); tot = int(c[-1])
        g = ((S7[i] + beta*R7[i]) > thr) & (drv[i] > athr)
        if tot == 1: m = c == 1; fpn += g[m].sum(); nn += m.sum()
        mt = (c == 0); fp_think += g[mt].sum(); nt += mt.sum()
        for t in np.where(tk == THINK_END)[0]:
            k = int(c[t])
            if k >= 4: hit += g[t-1]; nh += 1
            elif k >= 2: h23_ += g[t-1]; n23 += 1
    return fpn/nn, hit/nh, fp_think/nt, h23_/max(n23,1)
for beta in [0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0]:
    for thr in [2.0, 2.4, 2.8, 3.2, 3.6]:
        fp, ht, ft, h23r = gate_eval(beta, thr)
        print(f'beta {beta} thr {thr}: normalFP {fp:.4f} lateHit {ht:.3f} thinkFP {ft:.5f} close2-3 {h23r:.3f}')
```

The AND gate is clean on these texts. With score = w7 + 0.5·(h7 recency) above 3.2 and drive above 8, it fires before 91% of late closes (close #4 and later). It never fires in thinking or before 2nd/3rd closes, and fires at 0.02% of normal-answer positions; at threshold 3.6 there are no normal-answer false positives and 81% hits. Two caveats: these numbers include the texts the directions were fit on, and the h7 signal has to be carried to layer 23 where the drive gate lives.

Before building a two-stage circuit (layer-7 flag neuron writing a carrier direction), I'll check whether layer 23 can read the h7 score directly from its own input. This block fits a ridge readout of the h7 score from rms-normalized h23 (and h15) on training texts. It then evaluates the AND gate with the predicted score on held-out texts only, alongside the true h7 score on the same held-out texts. If the h23 readout keeps near-zero normal false positives with high late-close hits, a single new layer-23 neuron suffices. If not, I build the transport.

```python
# Hypothesis: the h7 flag score is still linearly readable at h23 (or h15), so one layer-23 neuron can implement the AND gate
def score7(i): return S7[i] + 0.5*R7[i]
def ridge_fit(li_src, ids, lam):
    A = []; yv = []
    for i in ids:
        Z = rmsn(np.asarray(H3[i, li_src, :lens[i]], dtype=np.float32)); A.append(Z[::3]); yv.append(score7(i)[::3])
    A = np.concatenate(A); yv = np.concatenate(yv)
    mu = A.mean(0); ym = yv.mean()
    G = (A-mu).T @ (A-mu); G += lam*np.trace(G)/1024*np.eye(1024)
    wv = np.linalg.solve(G, (A-mu).T @ (yv-ym)); return wv, mu, ym
def gate_eval_ids(ids, sc, thr, athr=8):
    fpn=nn=hit=nh=ft=nt=0
    for i in ids:
        n = lens[i]; tk = np.asarray(toks4[i,:n]); c = np.cumsum(tk == THINK_END); tot = int(c[-1])
        g = (sc[i] > thr) & (drv[i] > athr)
        if tot == 1: fpn += g[c==1].sum(); nn += (c==1).sum()
        ft += g[c==0].sum(); nt += (c==0).sum()
        for t in np.where(tk == THINK_END)[0]:
            if c[t] >= 4: hit += g[t-1]; nh += 1
    return fpn/max(nn,1), hit/max(nh,1), ft/max(nt,1), nn, nh
true_sc = {i: score7(i) for i in range(40)}
for thr in [3.2, 3.6]: print('TRUE h7 score test thr', thr, np.round(gate_eval_ids(te_ids, true_sc, thr), 4))
for src_h in [15, 23]:
    li_ = H3i.index(src_h)
    for lam in [0.01, 0.1]:
        wv, mu, ym = ridge_fit(li_, tr_ids, lam)
        pred = {i: (rmsn(np.asarray(H3[i, li_, :lens[i]], dtype=np.float32)) - mu) @ wv + ym for i in te_ids}
        r = np.corrcoef(np.concatenate([pred[i] for i in te_ids]), np.concatenate([true_sc[i] for i in te_ids]))[0,1]
        out = [np.round(gate_eval_ids(te_ids, pred, thr), 4) for thr in [3.0, 3.2, 3.6]]
        print(f'h{src_h} lam {lam}: test corr {r:.3f} | thr3.0/3.2/3.6 (normFP, lateHit, thinkFP, nn, nh):', out)
```

A single layer-23 neuron looks sufficient. A ridge readout of the h7 flag score from h23 has held-out correlation 0.92. ANDed with drive, it gives 0 false positives over 2,821 normal-answer positions and 0 in thinking, with 97% hits before late closes. These test texts are few (one strongly degenerate), so the gate must be confirmed in a teacher-forced pass after the edit.

Wiring matters. If the flag drives the gate (silu threshold) and the up row reads a drive that is around 0 at rest, the neuron is silent except when both are high. A drive readout that goes negative at baseline (3109's gate has a built-in offset) would reverse the action at flagged low-drive positions. The flag threshold also needs a near-constant bias direction at the layer-23 MLP input. This block does two checks. First, it looks for dimensions of rms-normalized h23 with small relative spread, including those 3109's builder used (31, 164, 476, 25, 357). Second, it measures the drive readout −u·x̂ in late pre-close positions, flagged positions, and normal answers.

```python
# Hypothesis: (1) h23 has near-constant normalized dims usable as a bias; (2) -u.x (drive) is ~0 at rest and large at late re-closes
Z23 = {i: rmsn(np.asarray(H3[i, L23i, :lens[i]], dtype=np.float32)) for i in range(40)}
allZ = np.concatenate([Z23[i][::5] for i in range(40)])
mu_d = allZ.mean(0); sd_d = allZ.std(0); cv = sd_d/np.abs(mu_d)
order = np.argsort(cv)
print('most constant dims (dim, mean, sd, cv):', [(int(j), round(float(mu_d[j]),2), round(float(sd_d[j]),3), round(float(cv[j]),3)) for j in order[:8]])
print('3109 delta dims:', [(j, round(float(mu_d[j]),2), round(float(sd_d[j]),3)) for j in [31,164,476,25,357]])
g23 = gam23.numpy()
qd = {i: -(Z23[i] * g23) @ u_np for i in range(40)}
pred23 = {}
wv23, mu23, ym23 = ridge_fit(L23i, tr_ids, 0.01)
for i in range(40): pred23[i] = (Z23[i] - mu23) @ wv23 + ym23
grp = {'normal1': [], 'pre_late': [], 'flag_any': [], 'think': []}
for i in range(40):
    n = lens[i]; tk = np.asarray(toks4[i,:n]); c = np.cumsum(tk == THINK_END); tot = int(c[-1])
    if tot == 1: grp['normal1'].append(qd[i][c==1])
    grp['think'].append(qd[i][c==0])
    grp['flag_any'].append(qd[i][(pred23[i] > 3.2) & (c >= 1)])
    for t in np.where(tk == THINK_END)[0]:
        if c[t] >= 4: grp['pre_late'].append(qd[i][t-1:t])
for k, v in grp.items():
    v = np.concatenate(v); print(f'{k:9s} n={len(v):6d} -u.x p1 {np.percentile(v,1):.3f} p50 {np.median(v):.3f} p90 {np.percentile(v,90):.3f} p99 {np.percentile(v,99):.3f}')
```

